#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093390 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n![](images/4a/4ad61501c6c9b94fa05be806aead4f481a1e1e2f1f34b8cfb346ce685424b1f9.svg)\n\nNotice that this equation contains a transformed sine function. \n\nRecall that the general solution of the elementary equation $\\sin{X} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & X & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed sine equation, we replace $X$ with the sine function's input, which is $x-\\dfrac{\\pi}3$ in this case. This gives the general solution\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{6n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =(1+2n)\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi (1+3n)}{3} & \\, & \\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $n=0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\leq x <2\\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$ and $x=\\pi.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = \\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d59e641-96b3-527c-98e2-41c5def454e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2683 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
